The Lempel-Ziv-Welch (LZW) algorithm is a lossless data compression algorithm that works by finding and replacing repeated patterns in the input data with shorter codes. The algorithm is based on the idea of dictionary compression, where a dictionary is used to map the input data to shorter codes. The algorithm is named after its inventors, Abraham Lempel, Jacob Ziv, and Terry Welch.
